EjisbLnWxFQGUTRjiHGoKY1oV5c"></div></figure><pre><code class=3D"hljs langua=
ge-python">Output: <span class=3D"hljs-literal">False</span>
</code></pre><h4 id=3D"constraints">Constraints:</h4><ul>
<li>Assume each integer on the board falls in the range of <code>[0, 9]</co=
de>.</li>
</ul></div>
<h2 id=3D"intuition">Intuition</h2>
<p>Our primary objective is to check every row, column, and each of the nin=
e 3x3 subgrids, for any duplicate numbers. Let's first discuss the mechanis=
m for finding duplicate elements, then look into how we can apply this to r=
ows, columns, and subgrids.</p>
<p><strong>Checking for duplicates</strong><br>
<!-- -->Let=E2=80=99s start by figuring out how to check for duplicates on =
a single row of the board:</p>
<figure class=3D"py-1"><div style=3D"display:flex;justify-content:center"><=
img alt=3D"Image represents a visual depiction of data transformation.  A 9=
x9 grid, resembling a partially completed Sudoku puzzle, is shown on the le=
ft.  The grid contains various single-digit numbers (1-9) arranged within i=
ts cells, with some cells remaining empty.  A specific row of the grid, hig=
hlighted in a light peach color, contains the numbers 1, 2, 5, 3, and 2. A =
rightward-pointing arrow connects this grid to a single-row, rectangular ar=
ray on the right. This array contains the same numbers (1, 2, 5, 3, 2) as t=
he highlighted row from the grid, arranged sequentially in individual cells=
, mirroring the order in the highlighted row. The image illustrates the ext=
raction of a specific row of data from a two-dimensional structure (the Sud=
oku grid) into a one-dimensional structure (the array), effectively demonst=
rating a data transformation or extraction process." loading=3D"lazy" width=
=3D"539" height=3D"249" decoding=3D"async" data-nimg=3D"1" style=3D"color:t=
ransparent" src=3D"https://bytebytego.com/images/courses/coding-patterns/ha=
sh-maps-and-sets/verify-sudoku-board/image-02-02-1-ZWN2WK7F.svg?dpl=3Ddpl_C=
EjisbLnWxFQGUTRjiHGoKY1oV5c"></div></figure>
<p>A naive way to do this is to take each number in the row and search the =
row to see if that number appears again. Performing a linear search for eac=
h number would result in a time complexity of <span class=3D"katex"><span c=
lass=3D"katex-mathml"><math xmlns=3D"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><s=
emantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=3D"false">(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2=
</mn></msup><mo stretchy=3D"false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=3D"app=
lication/x-tex">O(n^2)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=3D=
"katex-html" aria-hidden=3D"true"><span class=3D"base"><span class=3D"strut=
" style=3D"height:1.0641em;vertical-align:-0.25em"></span><span class=3D"mo=
rd mathnormal" style=3D"margin-right:0.02778em">O</span><span class=3D"mope=
n">(</span><span class=3D"mord"><span class=3D"mord mathnormal">n</span><sp=
an class=3D"msupsub"><span class=3D"vlist-t"><span class=3D"vlist-r"><span =
class=3D"vlist" style=3D"height:0.8141em"><span style=3D"top:-3.063em;margi=
n-right:0.05em"><span class=3D"pstrut" style=3D"height:2.7em"></span><span =
class=3D"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class=3D"mord mtight">2</sp=
an></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=3D"mclose">=
)</span></span></span></span> to check all numbers in one row, which is qui=
te time consuming.</p>
<p>We can use a <strong>hash set</strong> to improve the time complexity. B=
y using a hash set, we can keep track of which numbers were previously visi=
ted as we iterate through the row. When we encounter a new number, we can c=
heck if it's already in the set in <span class=3D"katex"><span class=3D"kat=
ex-mathml"><math xmlns=3D"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><m=
row><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=3D"false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=3D"false"=
>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=3D"application/x-tex">O(1)</annotation><=
/semantics></math></span><span class=3D"katex-html" aria-hidden=3D"true"><s=
pan class=3D"base"><span class=3D"strut" style=3D"height:1em;vertical-align=
:-0.25em"></span><span class=3D"mord mathnormal" style=3D"margin-right:0.02=
778em">O</span><span class=3D"mopen">(</span><span class=3D"mord">1</span><=
span class=3D"mclose">)</span></span></span></span> time. If it is, then it=
's a duplicate:</p>
